<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Shapsugs</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest of the self-governing Circassian peoples on the Black Sea coast, who overthrew their nobility and fought Russia until 1864.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Shapsugs lived along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast and inland hills between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/natukhaj">Natukhaj</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ubykhia">Ubykhs</a>, around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuapse">Tuapse</a>, and on the northern slopes towards the Kuban. They emerged as a distinct <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Adyghe</a> community of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/circassia">Circassia</a> by the 17th century; earlier history is not separately recorded.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the late 18th century Shapsug commoners rose against their nobles and defeated them and their <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-bzhedugh">Bzhedugh</a> allies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bziyuqo">Bziyuqo</a> in 1796. Afterwards they were governed by assemblies of elders and representatives of free households. They farmed, herded and traded with Ottoman ships. In the 1840s and 1850s they joined the assemblies that coordinated Circassian resistance.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> conquered their territory in 1864, and most Shapsugs were expelled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. A small community remained near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuapse">Tuapse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazarevskoye">Lazarevskoye</a>, the only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> left on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/shapsugs.jpg" alt="Shapsugs" width="320" height="121"><figcaption>Image: Yahya Kuadzhe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shapsug_Banner.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Adyghe (Shapsug dialect)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shapsugh, Shapsug free society</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=shapsugs" aria-label="Shapsugs on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5657 200 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shapsugs lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5955 888h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5977 456h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5977 456h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=shapsugs">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

